**Sec. 70001.** This section establishes that all amendments and repeals in Title VII refer to the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 unless otherwise specified. It explicitly states that Section 15 of the Internal Revenue Code, which governs the effect of rate changes, does not apply to any tax rate changes made by this title.

**Sec. 70101.** This section permanently extends the reduced individual income tax rates established by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act, removing the sunset provision that would have expired them after 2025. It modifies inflation adjustments for tax brackets higher than 12 percent and 22 percent to ensure consistent indexing. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70102.** This section permanently extends the increased standard deduction amounts, removing the 2025 sunset provision. It increases the base standard deduction amounts to $23,625 for single filers and $15,750 for married filing separately, with these figures indexed for inflation starting from 2025. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70103.** This section permanently terminates the suspension of personal exemptions but introduces a temporary $6,000 deduction for qualified individuals aged 65 or older for taxable years beginning before January 1, 2029. The deduction phases out by 6 percent for every dollar of modified adjusted gross income exceeding $75,000 for single filers or $150,000 for joint filers. Married individuals must file jointly to claim this deduction, and a valid Social Security number is required.

**Sec. 70104.** This section permanently extends the increased Child Tax Credit, raising the maximum credit amount from $2,000 to $2,200 per qualifying child. It mandates that both the taxpayer and the qualifying child must have valid Social Security numbers issued before the return due date to claim the credit. The refundable portion of the credit is capped at $1,400, with both the credit amount and refundable cap subject to inflation adjustments starting in 2025 and 2026 respectively.

**Sec. 70105.** This section permanently extends the 20 percent deduction for qualified business income (QBI) and increases the taxable income phase-in thresholds to $75,000 for single filers and $150,000 for joint filers. It establishes a minimum deduction of $400 for applicable taxpayers with at least $1,000 in aggregate qualified business income from active trades or businesses. These amounts are indexed for inflation beginning in 2027.

**Sec. 70106.** This section permanently increases the estate and gift tax exemption amount from $5,000,000 to $15,000,000, indexed for inflation starting in 2026. It removes the sunset provision that would have reduced the exemption back to pre-2018 levels after 2025. These changes apply to estates of decedents dying and gifts made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70107.** This section permanently extends the increased alternative minimum tax (AMT) exemption amounts, removing the 2025 sunset provision. It modifies the phaseout threshold for the AMT exemption to begin at 50 percent of the excess income rather than 25 percent. The inflation adjustment base year for the $1,000,000 exemption amount is updated to 2025.

**Sec. 70108.** This section permanently extends the limitation on the deduction for qualified residence interest, removing the 2025 sunset provision. It reinstates the deductibility of mortgage insurance premiums as qualified residence interest. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70109.** This section permanently extends the limitation on personal casualty loss deductions, which generally restricts deductions to losses attributable to federally declared disasters. It expands the definition of deductible casualty losses to include those resulting from State-declared disasters, as determined by the Governor or Mayor and the Secretary. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70110.** This section permanently terminates the suspension of miscellaneous itemized deductions, removing the 2025 sunset provision. It creates a specific exception allowing educators to deduct educator expenses as an itemized deduction, expanding the definition to include coaches and interscholastic sports administrators. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70111.** This section imposes a limitation on the tax benefit of itemized deductions for high-income taxpayers, reducing allowable itemized deductions by 2/37 of the lesser of the total itemized deductions or the amount of taxable income exceeding the threshold for the 37 percent tax bracket. This limitation applies after other itemized deduction limitations but does not affect the calculation of the qualified business income deduction. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70112.** This section permanently extends the exclusion for qualified transportation fringe benefits, removing the 2025 sunset provision. It eliminates the exclusion for qualified bicycle commuting reimbursements and adjusts the inflation indexing base year to 1997. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70113.** This section permanently extends the suspension of the moving expense deduction and exclusion for most taxpayers, removing the 2025 sunset provision. It creates an exception allowing members of the intelligence community (excluding Armed Forces members) who relocate due to assignment changes to deduct moving expenses and exclude employer reimbursements. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70114.** This section modifies the deduction for wagering losses by limiting the allowable deduction to 90 percent of the losses incurred during the taxable year. The deduction remains limited to the extent of wagering gains for the same year. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70115.** This section permanently extends the increased contribution limit for ABLE accounts, removing the 2025 sunset provision. It modifies the inflation adjustment mechanism for the annual contribution limit to use 1996 as the base year. These changes apply to contributions made after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70116.** This section permanently extends the saver’s credit for contributions to ABLE accounts, removing the 2025 sunset provision. It increases the maximum credit amount from $2,000 to $2,100 for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2026. The definition of qualified retirement savings contributions is updated to include ABLE account contributions.

**Sec. 70117.** This section permanently extends the permission to roll over funds from qualified tuition programs (529 plans) to ABLE accounts, removing the 2025 sunset provision. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70118.** This section permanently extends the tax exclusion for income earned by members of the Armed Forces performing services in the Sinai Peninsula. It expands the definition of qualified hazardous duty areas to include Kenya, Mali, Burkina Faso, and Chad, provided special hostile fire pay is authorized for those locations. These changes take effect on January 1, 2026.

**Sec. 70119.** This section permanently extends the exclusion from gross income for student loan discharges due to death or total and permanent disability. It expands the exclusion to include private education loans and requires the taxpayer to include their Social Security number on the tax return. These changes apply to discharges occurring after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70120.** This section permanently extends the limitation on the state and local tax (SALT) deduction, replacing the fixed $10,000 cap with a phased-in "applicable limitation amount." The cap starts at $40,000 in 2025, increases by 1 percent annually through 2029, and then reverts to $10,000 for taxable years beginning after 2029. The limitation phases out by 30 percent for modified adjusted gross income exceeding $500,000 for single filers or $1,000,000 for joint filers, but never falls below $10,000.

**Sec. 70201.** This section allows a deduction for qualified tips received by individuals in occupations that customarily received tips before 2025, capped at $25,000 annually. The deduction phases out by $100 for every $1,000 of modified adjusted gross income exceeding $150,000 for single filers or $300,000 for joint filers. The deduction is available to non-itemizers and terminates for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2028.

**Sec. 70202.** This section allows a deduction for qualified overtime compensation paid under the Fair Labor Standards Act, capped at $12,500 for single filers and $25,000 for joint filers. The deduction phases out by $100 for every $1,000 of modified adjusted gross income exceeding $150,000 for single filers or $300,000 for joint filers. The deduction is available to non-itemizers and terminates for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2028.

**Sec. 70203.** This section allows a deduction for interest paid on loans secured by an applicable passenger vehicle for personal use, capped at $10,000 annually. The deduction phases out by $200 for every $1,000 of modified adjusted gross income exceeding $100,000 for single filers or $200,000 for joint filers. The vehicle must be manufactured primarily for public roads, have a gross vehicle weight rating under 14,000 pounds, and undergo final assembly in the United States. This provision applies to indebtedness incurred after December 31, 2024, and terminates for taxable years beginning after December 31, 2028.

**Sec. 70204.** This section establishes "Trump Accounts," which are individual retirement accounts for individuals under age 18, funded by qualified rollover contributions or employer contributions up to $2,500 annually. It creates a pilot program providing a $1,000 refundable credit to parents for each eligible child born between 2025 and 2028, paid directly into the child's Trump Account. Contributions are limited to $5,000 annually (excluding exempt contributions), and funds must be invested in low-cost index funds tracking qualified indices like the S&P 500. The section appropriates $410 million to the Department of the Treasury for implementation.

**Sec. 70301.** This section permanently extends 100 percent bonus depreciation for qualified property acquired after January 19, 2025, removing the sunset provision. It allows taxpayers to elect reduced bonus depreciation rates of 40 percent or 60 percent for property placed in service in the first taxable year ending after January 19, 2025. The provision applies to property with recovery periods of 7 years or less and certain specified plants.

**Sec. 70302.** This section allows taxpayers to immediately deduct domestic research and experimental expenditures rather than amortizing them over 5 years, while foreign research expenditures remain subject to 15-year amortization. It permits an election to amortize certain domestic expenditures over at least 60 months. Small businesses meeting the gross receipts test may elect retroactive application to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2021.

**Sec. 70303.** This section modifies the business interest expense limitation by removing the pre-2022 exception for certain floor plan financing. It expands the definition of floor plan financing to include trailers and campers designed for temporary living quarters. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2024.

**Sec. 70304.** This section permanently extends the paid family and medical leave credit, allowing employers to claim a credit based on either wages paid to employees on leave or premiums paid for insurance policies providing such leave. It modifies the definition of qualifying employees to include those employed for at least 6 months and working at least 20 hours per week. The credit calculation excludes leave paid by state or local governments.

**Sec. 70305.** This section creates exceptions to the 50 percent limitation on business meal deductions for meals provided on fishing vessels, fish processing vessels, or at fish processing facilities located north of 50 degrees latitude outside metropolitan statistical areas. These changes apply to amounts paid or incurred after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70306.** This section increases the dollar limitation for expensing depreciable business assets under Section 179 from $1,000,000 to $2,500,000. It also increases the phase-out threshold for total asset acquisitions from $2,500,000 to $4,000,000. These amounts are indexed for inflation starting in 2025.

**Sec. 70307.** This section allows a 100 percent special depreciation allowance for qualified production property, defined as nonresidential real property used as an integral part of manufacturing, production, or refining activities. The property must be placed in service in the United States, have original use commencing with the taxpayer, and be constructed between January 19, 2025, and January 1, 2029. Recapture rules apply if the property ceases to be used for qualified production activities within 10 years.

**Sec. 70308.** This section increases the advanced manufacturing investment credit rate from 25 percent to 35 percent. These changes apply to property placed in service after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70309.** This section treats spaceports similarly to airports for purposes of exempt facility bond rules, allowing tax-exempt bonds to finance spaceport facilities. It defines spaceports as facilities used for manufacturing spacecraft, flight control, launch services, or crew transfer. The provision exempts spaceport ground leases from certain ownership requirements and allows manufacturing facilities within spaceports to qualify for tax-exempt financing.

**Sec. 70311.** This section modifies the foreign tax credit limitation by allocating specific deductions to foreign source net CFC tested income. It allocates the Section 250 deduction and related taxes to such income, while excluding interest expense and research expenditures from allocation unless directly allocable. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70312.** This section increases the deemed paid foreign tax credit percentage for taxes attributable to tested income from 80 percent to 90 percent. It disallows a foreign tax credit for 10 percent of foreign income taxes paid with respect to distributions of previously taxed net CFC tested income. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025, with the disallowance applying to taxes paid after June 28, 2025.

**Sec. 70313.** This section modifies the sourcing rules for income from the sale of inventory produced in the United States and sold through foreign branches. It treats up to 50 percent of such income as foreign source if attributable to a foreign office or fixed place of business. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70321.** This section modifies the deduction for foreign-derived deduction eligible income (FDDEI) and net CFC tested income by reducing the deduction percentage for FDDEI from 37.5 percent to 33.34 percent and for net CFC tested income from 50 percent to 40 percent. It removes the coordination rule with the foreign tax credit limitation. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70322.** This section expands the definition of deduction eligible income to include income and gain from the sale or disposition of intangible property and depreciable property. It limits the expenses and deductions subtracted from gross income to those properly allocable, excluding interest and research expenditures. These changes apply to sales occurring after June 16, 2025, and taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70323.** This section renames "global intangible low-taxed income" (GILTI) to "net CFC tested income" and "foreign-derived intangible income" (FDII) to "foreign-derived deduction eligible income." It repeals the tax-free deemed return on foreign investments component of the GILTI calculation. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70331.** This section increases the base erosion minimum tax rate from 10 percent to 10.5 percent. It removes the special rate for banks and registered securities dealers, applying the general rate to all applicable taxpayers. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70341.** This section coordinates the business interest expense limitation with interest capitalization provisions, requiring the limitation to apply to business interest regardless of whether it is deducted or capitalized. It prioritizes the application of the allowed interest amount to capitalized interest before deducted interest. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70342.** This section modifies the definition of adjusted taxable income for the business interest expense limitation to include amounts included in gross income under sections 951(a), 951A(a), and 78. It also includes the portion of deductions allowed under sections 245A(a) and 250(a)(1)(B) attributable to such inclusions. These changes apply to taxable years beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70351.** This section permanently extends the look-thru rule for related controlled foreign corporations, removing the 2025 sunset provision. These changes apply to taxable years of foreign corporations beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70352.** This section repeals the election for a 1-month deferral in determining the taxable year of specified foreign corporations. It requires specified foreign corporations to align their taxable year with the required year ending after November 30, 2025. Transition rules address the change in taxable year and allocation of foreign taxes.

**Sec. 70353.** This section restores the limitation on downward attribution of stock ownership in applying constructive ownership rules for controlled foreign corporations. It introduces a new category of "foreign controlled United States shareholders" and "foreign controlled foreign corporations" to ensure certain foreign-controlled entities are subject to subpart F and GILTI rules. These changes apply to taxable years of foreign corporations beginning after December 31, 2025.

**Sec. 70354.** This section modifies the pro rata share rules for subpart F income and net CFC tested income to attribute income based on the period during which the shareholder owned stock and the corporation was a controlled foreign corporation. It clarifies the taxable year of inclusion for shareholders. These changes apply to taxable years of foreign corporations beginning after December 31, 2025, with transition rules for dividends paid before that date. [end of text]


